// A function may continue into a chain:
//
//     name(params) { ... } chain (params) { ... } return TYPE expr;
//
// A chain is a literal continuation, not a call: the parameters, the imports
// and every local declared so far are still in scope, and control falls into
// it. So the segments are collected into the one body the function already
// has, and nothing downstream -- lowering, the backends, the harness -- ever
// sees a seam.
//
// What a chain is *for* is the three-actions-per-block limit: a block that is
// full continues into the next segment instead of being split into a helper
// that is called once and never again. The limit is unchanged; where the work
// goes is what changed.

parse_segments(int[] pos, int[] params) {
  int[] body = parse_block(pos);
  chain_loop(pos, params, body);
} return int[] body;
given decl_tokens ([8], []):([5, 13])
given decl_tokens ([8], [0]):([5, 13])

// `chain` is a keyword (lex/chars/classify/ident_kw.id), so this reads the
// token text rather than a kind: the cursor sits just past the closing brace
// parse_block consumed.
chain_loop(int[] pos, int[] params, int[] body) {
  while(cur_text(pos) == "chain") {
    take_chain(pos, params, body);
  }
} return void;
given decl_tokens ([33], [], []):() then check_failed:(0)
given decl_tokens ([35], [], []):() then check_failed:(0)

// The parameters are restated for the reader, and must match exactly -- they
// name the same values, so a restatement that disagrees is a mistake about
// what is in scope rather than a second signature.
take_chain(int[] pos, int[] params, int[] body) {
  advance(pos);
  int[] restated = parse_params(pos, "param");
  chain_tail(pos, params, restated, body);
} return void;
